From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:51:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] media: verisilicon: raise the VPU981 AV1 frame size limit

The VPU981 AV1 decoder declares 4096x2304 as its maximum frame size, and
so do the formats on both ends of its capture path.  Nothing in the
driver derives that number.  Every buffer the decoder sizes for a frame
scales with the frame: the deblocking, CDEF, superres and loop-restoration
column buffers are computed from the frame height and the tile column
count in tiles_reallocate(), and the motion vector storage from the
superblock count in hantro_av1_mv_size().  The picture dimension registers
carry no ceiling near it either -- av1_pic_width_in_cbs and
av1_pic_height_in_cbs are thirteen bits of eight-pixel units, which spans
far past 8K in both axes.

Raise the limit to 8192x4352, which is the largest frame an AV1 level can
describe.  Both of the decoder's format lists move, because either one
alone silently clamps the picture: hantro_try_fmt() applies the
constraints of whichever format the CAPTURE queue holds, so leaving the
postprocessor's NV12/NV15/P010 entries at 4K would accept an 8K stream on
the coded queue and hand back a 4096x2304 frame with no error anywhere.
The decoder's own NV12_4L4/NV15_4L4 entries move for the same reason on
the path that skips the postprocessor.

The tile-count bounds are unchanged and stay correct: they are counts, not
dimensions, and their limits come from the AV1 maxima and from the size of
the tile_info scratch.  A larger frame does need more tiles -- a tile is at
most 4096 samples wide and 4096x2304 in area, so an 8K frame needs at least
four -- but that is far below the 128 the scratch holds.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
